WEBVTT

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.570
Here, soft-boiled.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.800
It turned out good today.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.110
Thanks.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.830
Our shifts are the same today, right?

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:21.500
Yeah.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.140
I'll make something 
for dinner tonight, then.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.480
Wanna eat together?

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.130
Yay!

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.570
I wish I could know 
what Ban-chan's feeling.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.140
When are they going to get back 
to open waters?

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.680
I'm sure they can 
when the time comes.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.620
Like the sea-turtle hatchlings did.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.500
You're right.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:23.200
<i>Good morning, everyone.</i>

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:29.080
<i>I, Aquarium Tingarla director Hoshino Akira, 
have an important announcement to make.</i>

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:32.510
Our aquarium will be launching 
a new research facility,

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:35.160
opening two years from now.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.970
<i>And it's called the USTD Aquatope Project.</i>

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:39.970
<b>USTD Aquatope Project</b>

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.720
"USTD"...

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:47.310
Umi-yan and his Shochu pops: Totally Devoted?

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:47.900
Really?!

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:48.770
You don't have any.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:55.400
<i>USTD stands for "Under the Sea with
sea creatures: Totally Devoted!"</i>

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.670
I was right about the "totally devoted" part.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:02:59.900
Seriously?

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.930
<i>The Aquatope will be a big project,</i>

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:05.610
<i>involving not just research 
on species that we raise,</i>

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:11.070
<i>but also exploration of uncharted
waters and investigation</i>

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:17.280
<i>into environmental issues 
affecting marine life.</i>

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.640
<i>Climate change, microplastics, 
overfishing and many other factors</i>

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:27.180
<i>are changing the map 
of the entire biosphere.</i>

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.720
<i>How do we respond to these changes?</i>

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.760
<i>What can we do for the future 
of these critters?</i>

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:38.960
<i>I want us to go beyond an aquarium 
to ponder these questions.</i>

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:40.320
Magnificent.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.050
<i>Now, we're looking for two people 
to make up the first crew of this project.</i>

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:48.290
<i>The crew will have plenty to study</i>

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:53.040
<i>during their two-year training course 
at Waihana Aquarium in Hawaii.</i>

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:57.480
Isn't Waihana where the director was before?

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.670
It's the most progressive aquarium 
in the world.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.640
<i>If you're interested, please write a report 
detailing your reasons for wanting to join.</i>

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:09.260
<i>It's time for you to step forward!</i>

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:10.930
<b>Facility Broadcast</b>

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:14.520
You young'uns ought to go for it.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.350
I'm very interested.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.150
Overseas? Nope.

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:21.220
I'm kind of interested, too, but...

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:25.780
Jeez.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:29.160
I don't want to leave 
our penguins behind right now.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.500
They're finally warming up to us.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.290
It does sound fun, though.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.740
Kaoru-san, what about you?

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.500
I'm going to go for it, of course.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:36.680
What about you?

00:04:40.150 --> 00:04:42.240
The director's Aquatope?

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.510
It sounds really cool.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:44.620
Yeah.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:49.390
I hadn't really thought 
about oceanic issues until now.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:53.120
Imagine being able to train 
at an aquarium in Hawaii.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:54.940
Wanna go together?

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:57.100
Yeah!

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:02.020
Then again, I don't think I can ask for that 
after I left without notice.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:04.650
Did you want to go?

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:06.560
Nah.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:08.160
Not really.

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:11.370
You might get to go back 
to being an attendant, after all.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:13.700
You're interviewing 
with the director tomorrow, right?

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.210
Oh, yeah.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.670
Right, Choko's coming back tomorrow.

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:21.530
I can't wait to see them.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.060
<b>I</b>

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:41.820
<b>Overseas</b>

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:45.750
<i>I'd have to be away from Kukuru 
for two years.</i>

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:52.830
There's an opening 
for a position as attendant.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:55.340
Do you wish to transfer?

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.740
How is work in marketing?

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.220
It's difficult.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.340
The assistant director has given up on me.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:09.750
He brought up the transfer 
the other day, too.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:13.020
He probably doesn't think 
I'm cut out for marketing.

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:15.680
Are you sure about that?

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:23.240
Plankton.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:24.360
Y-Yes?

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:30.430
About that plan you had, to invite local kids 
to the new area opening...

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:31.550
We got the go-ahead.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:32.700
Really?

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:35.020
Inform the appropriate departments.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:35.740
Yes, sir!

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:37.200
Thank you!

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:51.880
Do you know what Suwa-san, the assistant
director, was doing before he came here?

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:53.970
I heard he worked at a bank.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:55.070
Yeah.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:59.370
He was in charge of an aquarium 
that had gone under.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:04.520
And having learned about the sad fate 
of the animals after the aquarium closed,

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:07.530
he was wracked with guilt.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:11.030
The first time I met him, he said,

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:14.710
"I don't ever want 
to go through that again."

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:19.960
He came here to Tingarla 
to protect the animals.

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:29.530
You look pretty gloomy.

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:31.700
Does that mean you didn't
get the attendant position?

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:35.160
Nah, he just asked if I wanted the job.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:36.220
Oh.

00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:39.310
You and Karin-san are still rivals, then.

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:41.470
Being an attendant would be nice,

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:44.720
but what about the overseas training 
for the Aquatope project?

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.730
I'm interested, but first I have 
to learn the basics of being an attendant.

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:52.400
I came to Tingarla because I wanted 
to become an attendant, after all.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:53.240
Yeah.

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:57.330
Even if it doesn't work out this time, 
I might get tapped next time.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:00.190
You're so positive, Karin-san.

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:04.040
I heard Kaoru-san threw in her hat 
for the overseas training.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:05.550
It's a perfect opportunity.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:06.750
I really want to go.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:08.410
I'm rooting for you.

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:10.910
You've always been interested.

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:11.770
Yeah.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:18.540
Miyazawa-san.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:19.220
Yes?

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:21.980
What are you going to do 
about the overseas training?

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.250
What do you mean?

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:27.290
You said you were interested 
in environmental issues.

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:29.980
You might never get 
an opportunity like this again.

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:34.490
I'm interested, 
but I don't want to leave Kukuru.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:36.080
Ah.

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.270
Well, it's up to you to decide in the end.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:40.120
Bye.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:47.140
Kukuru.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:49.500
Kai.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:50.670
What's up?

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:51.840
I came to pick up my stuff.

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:54.990
But then I heard Choko's coming today.

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:56.220
I'm going to say "hi" 
before I go home.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:56.840
Ah.

00:08:57.570 --> 00:08:59.840
How's your dad doing?

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:02.400
He's getting surgery 
at the end of the month.

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:03.730
I see.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:05.940
I'll go see him sometime.

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:07.290
Thanks.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:10.180
You're still in marketing?

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:14.440
I thought you would be the one 
taking my position.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:16.110
Yeah...

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:27.830
I missed you, Choko, everyone!

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:30.870
Everyone remembers you, huh?

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:34.680
Everyone's in good health.

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:37.740
Uozu Aquarium let us know 
that there were no issues

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:40.360
while they were taking care of them.

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:41.560
One of Gramps's connections?

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:42.560
Yes.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.890
He knows people all over the country.

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:47.640
Beware the Gama Gama Gramps Network.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:49.530
G'day.

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:51.240
Hey, it's Choko.

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:52.150
They haven't changed.

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:53.950
It's been a long journey for them.

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:56.330
Let's keep an eye on them here 
for the next few days.

00:09:56.330 --> 00:09:58.240
They can come outside after that.

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:00.740
Okay, understood!

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:07.000
<b>The Future of the Aquarium</b>

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:13.000
<b>Attendants</b>

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:13.000
<b>Penguin equipment</b>

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:13.000
There.

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.430
<b>Misakino Kukuru</b>

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:18.430
<b>1PM, right? Okay! 
I hope Choko's well.</b>

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:18.430
<b>You're having dinner with me tonight, too, right? 
Is there anything you want?</b>

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:18.430
<b>I have overtime >_< Sorry, we'll have 
to have dinner separately tonight!</b>

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:18.430
<b>Sorry...</b>

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.120
<b>What I Want to Learn Abroad</b>

00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:54.950
I don't see you here 
by yourself very often.

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:58.250
I can't have Fuuka cook
for me all the time.

00:10:58.650 --> 00:11:01.230
She must be worried about you.

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:02.170
Why?

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:06.300
I mean, you hole yourself up in Gama Gama 
and run away and stuff.

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:09.480
That's who you are, though.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:10.970
You're honest.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:12.390
I like that about you.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:14.480
Thanks.

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:19.780
Did you want to go?

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:21.740
Nah.

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:23.240
Not really.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:32.250
<i>I found myself in this job 
after I met someone last year.</i>

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:39.110
<i>That person is always thinking about 
what she can do for the animals.</i>

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:49.020
<i>That was also something that was valued 
at the small aquarium where she was.</i>

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.010
<b>Morning!</b>

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:46.010
<b>I have a lot to do, 
so I'll see you at work!</b>

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:53.630
<i>The other day, I had the opportunity 
to see sea turtles hatch.</i>

00:11:53.630 --> 00:11:57.080
<i>It was very heartwarming, 
but at the same time,</i>

00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:02.400
<i>I learned about the severe
environmental changes affecting life,</i>

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.620
<i>and I decided that I wanted 
to learn more.</i>

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:16.080
Thanks for coming so early in the morning.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:17.120
Those of you here

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:20.050
are the ones who made it 
past the initial screening based on your reports.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:24.550
We'll be using the rest of the time 
until opening to make our final decisions.

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:27.470
As we informed you before, the subject is

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:30.140
any species that you would like to introduce.

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:34.110
You may use any means you wish 
to make a presentation of

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:38.730
what you would like to study 
about this species in this project.

00:12:39.070 --> 00:12:40.910
Higa-kun, you're up first.

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:41.900
Okay.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:46.120
This is the fish 
that I would like to present.

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:47.910
Please try it first.

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:52.330
I didn't think we'd be served food.

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:54.250
Thank you!

00:12:56.810 --> 00:12:58.150
What do you think?

00:12:58.150 --> 00:12:59.800
It's delicious.

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:02.840
It's rich for a white-meat fish.

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:04.630
That's eeltail catfish.

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:07.050
It's poisonous!

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:08.540
Not to worry.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:10.560
It's been prepared by a professional.

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:15.230
Also, this isn't our eeltail catfish, is it?

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:17.770
Of course not.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:19.360
I wouldn't eat them without permission.

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:24.950
As you the chief noted, 
eeltail catfish has poisons.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:29.450
<b>Striped puffer</b>

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:29.450
<b>Eeltail catfish</b>

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:29.450
<b>Fugu sashimi</b>

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:29.450
<b>Tempura</b>

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:29.000
The human appetite knows no bounds

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:32.200
and drives us to discover new ways 
to cook even poisonous fish.

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:32.220
<b>Inimicus japonicus</b>

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:32.220
<b>Red stingray</b>

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:32.220
<b>Karaage</b>

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:32.220
<b>Stingray fillet</b>

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:35.760
But it's known to everyone here 
that market demands

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:40.620
to satisfy this appetite are beginning 
to affect marine ecosystems.

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:43.360
If we don't come up 
with a new scheme soon,

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:46.920
we will soon have consumed 
all the fish in the world.

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.720
Coral reefs are not only 
a resource for tourism,

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:53.040
but also serve an important role 
as a buffer to open waters.

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:54.990
Jellyfish are popular now,

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:58.350
but it took a lot of research before
we could show them off the way we do.

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:02.400
Likewise, it may soon be possible 
to raise species that are currently difficult to.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:05.240
The robber clam is now
classified as endangered.

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:09.990
In my hometown of Miyako City, 
they were enjoyed by the name "Makugan."

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:13.990
Once an animal goes extinct, 
we can never see them again.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:17.750
There is a lot in Okinawa 
that we must not lose.

00:14:18.250 --> 00:14:22.250
I'd like to dedicate my life 
to look for ways to conserve them

00:14:22.250 --> 00:14:24.870
for the next one hundred—
one thousand—years.

00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:28.380
Well done.

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:30.470
Miyazawa-san, you're last.

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:32.070
Where should we go?

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:33.580
Actually...

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:43.040
Why are we here?

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:43.770
Right?

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:45.940
Where's the dolphin?

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:50.950
Hello, everyone!

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:52.890
She tripped.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:54.490
A bottlenose dolphin.

00:14:54.830 --> 00:14:55.740
What...

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:57.370
Does she think she's doing?

00:14:57.370 --> 00:14:58.870
It's the dolphin!

00:14:58.870 --> 00:14:59.830
Dolphin!

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:01.350
It tripped!

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:03.080
I'm fine.

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:06.340
I want to introduce you 
to my friend.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:09.470
Ban-chan the bottlenose dolphin.

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:10.490
There!

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:11.660
The dolphin!

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:12.640
It's so cute.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:15.030
How old do you think Ban-chan is?

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:18.810
A hundred!

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:21.020
Three! Same as Nii-chan.

00:15:21.470 --> 00:15:22.840
Thank you.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:25.340
Ban-chan is still a kid dolphin.

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:26.650
Probably about a year old.

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:28.650
Still a baby.

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:31.470
Even at one, 
Ban-chan is a good swimmer.

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:34.660
They normally spend their time 
in a big family.

00:15:35.070 --> 00:15:39.660
They chat with each other through whistles.

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:42.520
"A shark's coming! Look out!"

00:15:42.520 --> 00:15:45.310
"There are some good sardines over there!"

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:46.500
Stuff like that.

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:51.510
But a little while ago, 
Ban-chan came here all alone.

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:54.010
Isn't their mommy with them?

00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:55.440
She isn't.

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:58.260
Why do you think Ban-chan is here alone?

00:15:58.890 --> 00:16:00.980
Who thinks they're lost?

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:04.020
Okay, thank you.

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:09.100
Who thinks they ran away 
from a big, scary fish?

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:12.950
Who thinks they came to hang out 
with friends?

00:16:13.340 --> 00:16:14.930
Me!

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:16.320
Thank you.

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:18.940
We all have all kinds of thoughts,

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:23.320
but no one really knows 
why Ban-chan came here.

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:27.360
I wish I could talk to Ban-chan.

00:16:27.360 --> 00:16:29.630
Aren't their mom and dad worried?

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:31.150
Yeah...

00:16:31.150 --> 00:16:33.190
I'm sure they miss their family.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:35.790
But Ban-chan and the other dolphins

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:39.310
seem to have a lot of abilities 
that humans don't.

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:44.250
So I believe they'll eventually find 
their way back to their family.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:47.440
I hope you all join me 
in watching over Ban-chan.

00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:48.510
Okay!

00:16:48.510 --> 00:16:50.440
Bye!

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:58.780
<b>USTD Trainee Candidate</b>

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:58.780
<b>Shimabukuro Kaoru</b>

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:58.780
<b>USTD Aquatope Project</b>

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:58.780
<b>Motivation</b>

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:58.780
<b>- To protect our beautiful natural resources 
for future generations -</b>

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:58.780
Shall we go with Shimabukuro-san, then?

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:03.630
Her report was detailed, 
and her presentation was passionate.

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:04.380
Yes.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:05.540
I agree.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:07.950
I nominate Higa-kun for the second.

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:09.280
I agree.

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:11.920
He has postgraduate research experience.

00:17:12.270 --> 00:17:14.340
Although he can be quite unique.

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:17.340
Miyazawa-san was quite unique, too.

00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:20.840
She was the only one 
who left the aquarium.

00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:22.970
It seemed like a kids' show.

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:25.850
I don't know if she's actually ready 
to work at a research institution.

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:34.860
<b>USTD Trainee Candidate</b>

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:34.860
<b>Miyazawa Fuuka</b>

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:34.860
<b>USTD Aquatope Project</b>

00:17:25.850 --> 00:17:34.860
<b>What I Want to Learn Overseas</b>

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:30.160
The theme of the presentation was 
"A species that you want to introduce."

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:31.240
I think she was the only one

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:34.860
who had thought through whom she wanted 
to introduce that species to.

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:37.260
That being children?

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:39.850
Perhaps she wanted children

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:44.290
to appreciate marine life, 
as they'll be the ones inheriting the future.

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:47.960
But the Aquatope isn't a kids' facility.

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:49.900
That's not true.

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:52.460
Anything goes, with <i>USTD.</i>

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:55.820
She was asking a lot of questions.

00:17:55.820 --> 00:17:59.090
Maybe she wanted the children 
to ask questions.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:03.320
She's still a child in many ways at this job.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:06.390
So maybe she's asking those
questions of herself, too.

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:10.340
There are all kinds of
creatures at an aquarium.

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:14.650
They take on forms and ways of being 
that are foreign to human common sense.

00:18:15.230 --> 00:18:19.610
The children who come to an aquarium 
have their imaginations

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:23.540
inspired by being exposed 
to all kinds of strange things.

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:27.710
That may also lead to being imaginative 
about other people.

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:31.290
Don't you think that would make 
for a very kind world?

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:34.550
I sometimes dream...

00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:38.520
that some of the children 
who come to the aquarium

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:44.430
will go on to think deeply about life 
and discover new things.

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:48.500
They might solve environmental issues someday.

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:52.940
They might get to see species 
that have gone extinct.

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:57.690
They might come to be able 
to communicate with the animals someday.

00:18:58.180 --> 00:19:05.400
Don't you get excited that our work 
might someday be connected to such a place?

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:17.500
I heard.

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:19.290
Congratulations.

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:24.060
You were chosen for the
overseas training, weren't you?

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:29.480
I can't go.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:32.860
I can't leave you.

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:39.490
I decided...

00:19:39.490 --> 00:19:41.740
to stay in marketing.

00:19:43.300 --> 00:19:44.990
Not as an attendant?

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:49.740
I want to gain the skills to protect the animals 
through my marketing work.

00:19:50.230 --> 00:19:53.960
I think there's still more I can learn 
from the assistant director.

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:57.550
I'm sorry I kept worrying you.

00:19:57.840 --> 00:19:59.980
I turned you into my big sister.

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:01.760
That's not true.

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:03.020
No.

00:20:03.020 --> 00:20:04.880
I was being a baby.

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:09.270
When Gama Gama was going away, 
when I was stuck in my marketing work,

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:12.100
when I ran off to the islands 
without telling anyone...

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:15.300
You were always next to me.

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:21.130
But I can't keep depending on you.

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:25.200
So you can go. 
Don't worry about me.

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:26.160
No!

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:30.850
I've made it until now 
because you were next to me!

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:33.700
I'm the one who needs you.

00:20:34.820 --> 00:20:39.700
But I can't give up 
on studying more, either.

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:51.370
I'll be your big sister this time.

00:20:52.940 --> 00:20:54.740
Your big sister will be waiting here.

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:58.110
So go on ahead.

00:20:59.030 --> 00:21:01.560
Study lots while you're there.
